</p>

<h2>The actual reason: different zero points</h2>
<p>
  Most units share a zero point by definition — zero meters is zero meters is zero feet, however you measure it. Temperature scales don't. 0°C is the freezing point of water, a specific physical temperature. 0°F is a different, colder physical temperature (about −17.8°C), based on a reference point Daniel Fahrenheit chose in 1724 involving a brine solution. Since the two scales don't agree on where zero is, you can't convert between them with a single multiplication — you need to shift by an offset as well as scale by a ratio.
</p>
<p>
  The formula: <strong>°F = (°C × 9/5) + 32</strong>. The 9/5 accounts for Fahrenheit degrees being smaller than Celsius degrees (180 Fahrenheit degrees span the same range as 100 Celsius degrees, between freezing and boiling water — hence 180/100 = 9/5). The +32 shifts for the different zero point. Skip the +32 and every conversion is wrong by a constant 32 degrees, however carefully you get the ratio right — a common manual-conversion mistake.
</p>

<h2>Kelvin: the one that's actually simple</h2>
<p>
  Kelvin uses exactly the same size degree as Celsius — a 1-degree change means the same thing in both scales — so converting between them is pure addition, no scaling required: <strong>K = °C + 273.15</strong>. The offset exists because Kelvin's zero point is absolute zero, the coldest temperature physically possible (roughly −273.15°C), rather than water's freezing point. This is why Kelvin is the standard in physics and chemistry: it has a genuine, physically meaningful zero, which makes ratios ("twice as hot") actually meaningful in Kelvin in a way they aren't in Celsius or Fahrenheit (20°C is not "twice as hot" as 10°C in any physical sense; 200 K actually is half the absolute temperature of 400 K).
</p>

<h2>Why this matters beyond trivia</h2>
<p>
  Any conversion tool — including a calculator built by hand in a spreadsheet — that treats temperature the same way it treats length or weight (pure multiplication) will produce systematically wrong results. It's a useful example of a broader point: not every unit relationship is a simple ratio, and building (or trusting) a converter means checking whether the underlying quantities actually share a zero point, not assuming they do.
</p>

